course: dictionary
lesson: 11919
title: Short words and slang: caff
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are shortened so they are quicker to say. Caff is a short, informal way of saying café, and it is used mostly in Britain. Context gives you the clue: «We had egg and chips at the local caff after football.» Egg, chips and football tell you it is a place to eat.
explain: Read the rest of the sentence before you guess. «The caff was small and cheap, and the tea came in thick white mugs.» Small, cheap and tea tell you what kind of place it is. Swap your meaning into the sentence: "We had egg and chips at the local small, cheap café" still makes sense, so caff means café.
explain: Informal words are fine in a story or in chat with friends, but you would not usually use them in a formal letter or an exam essay. Writers use caff to make a scene sound real and British. When you meet it, picture a simple, busy place where people eat.
fact: Caff is a short, informal form of café.
fact: Caff is mostly used in British English.
fact: A caff is a small, simple, cheap café, often serving fried breakfasts, tea and sandwiches.
fact: A «greasy spoon» is another everyday name for this kind of café.
fact: The plural is «caffs»: two or more of these small cafés.
exercise: «We had egg and chips at the local caff after football.» What is a caff? | A football pitch | *A small, cheap café serving simple food | A shop that sells only sweets | A large, fancy hotel with a swimming pool
exercise: «The caff was small and cheap, and the tea came in thick white mugs.» Find and copy the short word for café. | mugs | *caff | tea | cheap
exercise: Which place is most likely to be called a caff? | *A busy little café serving fried breakfasts and tea | A sports stadium with thousands of fans | A palace with gold ceilings | A library full of quiet readers
test: «Dad ordered a bacon sandwich and a cup of tea in the greasy caff.» What does greasy caff most likely mean? | *A simple, cheap café where people eat | A cold ice rink | A quiet garden with flowers | A place for dancing
test: Why would a writer not use caff in a formal school letter? | It is a Scottish word for rain | It means a palace | *It is informal slang, not a formal word | It is a number
exam: What is caff short for, and where is it mostly used? | Carpet, in France | Cabbage, in Australia | Cafeteria, in America | *Café, in Britain
